<article class="env-corollary" id="04LF">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04LF">Corollary <span data-tag="04LF">9.6.1.19</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category, let $W$ be a collection of morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ which is closed under isomorphism, and let $\kappa $ be an uncountable regular cardinal. Then a morphism $f$ of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ belongs to the $\kappa $-small transfinite closure of $W$ if and only if $f$ is either an isomorphism or a $\kappa $-small transfinite composition of morphisms of $W$. </p>
</article>
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Assume that $f$ belongs to the $\kappa $-small transfinite closure of $W$; we will show that $f$ is either an isomorphism or a $\kappa $-small transfinite composition of morphisms of $W$ (the converse is clear, since the $\kappa $-small transfinite closure of $W$ contains all isomorphisms: see Example <a data-tag="04L3" href="/tag/04L3">9.6.1.8</a>). Let $W^{+}$ be the union of $W$ with the collection of all identity morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Applying Proposition <a data-tag="04LC" href="/tag/04LC">9.6.1.16</a>, we see that $f$ is a $\kappa $-small transfinite composition of morphisms of $W^{+}$. If $f$ is not an isomorphism, then Proposition <a data-tag="04LE" href="/tag/04LE">9.6.1.18</a> guarantees that $f$ is a $\kappa $-small transfinite composition of morphisms which belong to $W^{+}$ and are not isomorphisms, and therefore belong to $W$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
